\f1\fs22 It was not now or never for Tony, it was simply never. Soon, the elevator would bing, open, and swallow him whole, together with his steam cleaner and his cart with the medical-waste bag of suicide goo discreetly closeted inside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony felt like suicide goo. He and the hapless Travis Teh were equals. As the elevator dropped the 216 storeys down to the Cloudborne street, away from the office of angels, the dominion of Sarah, he grew lonelier. As fast as the elevator went, Tony\'92s heart plummeted faster.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Out on the street, under the wheeze of the Layer, Tony\'92s shoulders slumped and he crumpled. After Sarah, he doubted he\'92d ever stand upright again.Tony bunted his cart out the service doors on the lower ground level of the Tower of Sarah, morosely and fatalistically whistling a tune that, in countless other universes, would be known as a song called "Puppet on a String." In a spooky coincidence that hinted at the true nature of the multiverse, Tony sang a line, You can do <the rest of the line redacted for security reasons>.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Given enough free time, he may well have channelled the whole song, but his embittered thoughts surrendered to cursing the Airborne, whose engineered lack of care created the oppressively constant cloud over his head, which separated, both physically and symbolically, the privileged from the under-privileged, the entitled from the mis-titled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>One day, he mused, there\'92d be a revolution. An army of champion troubadours\'97or was it matadors or pompadours?\'97would rise up and \'85 what? Tony could never decide, as the voltage of his dim imagination fizzled out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony grunted as he pushed the skidding cart up the ramp, slippery with a film of condensed atmospheric chemicals. Later, he would have to clean the cart wheels and axles from the sticky residue that gummed up everything below the Layer. The weather had been aggressive all week; his van would need its third wash to slow down its worsening corrosion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At ground level, he looked up, as if he possessed cloud-piercing vision, as if he knew which windows on which floor were Sarah\'92s office, as if she was at those windows, basking in a halo of warm, glorious sunlight, looking beneficently down upon him. About halfway to where he parked his van, at the last point before the Tower of Sarah would be obscured by stunted Cloudborne buildings, Tony turned and looked forlornly for the last time in her direction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He didn\'92t see the body on the pitted cement. His cart jammed into the poor sod, lurched out of his hands, and threatened to topple. Tony snatched at the cart, and it fell the other way. He overbalanced and toppled onto the body. Embarrassed, he looked furtively about. He needn\'92t have bothered; passers-by, with noses up and eyes straight, strode elliptical paths around the scene of death and clumsiness, and dispersed into the sabulous morning haze.While no one would credit him with intelligence, Tony possessed a singular, acute street-urchin cunning. Stitching together the weave of simple observations, he would always spot the game, whereas the more educated and intellectually blessed would have loftier things on their presumptive minds, and thereby remain oblivious to things bleedingly obvious to him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For instance, this man wore an expensive, new off-black suit (Tony would never know the colour was midnight blue, being unfamiliar with the colour of a crisp night sky above the Layer), yet the man\'92s hands were calloused, his fingers oddly bent, his nails filthy. Figuring there could be out-of-place valuables on the dead man\'92s person, and with the brazen face of a man living a largely invisible life, Tony chiselled through the man\'92s pockets, shoes, and underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hey, you!\'92 A voice of petty authority came from behind Tony\'92s cart.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony rose and patted down his overalls. Looming down on him were a Coroner\'92s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I was just about to call you guys,\'92 Tony said with a sycophantic tone. \'91I think he\'92s been dead a while. He\'92s cold.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And how is it you know he\'92s cold?\'92 the body-snatcher asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I, ah \'85 I\'92m sorry I didn\'92t see him! I \'85 I ran over him, with my cart.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, you have a heat-sensing cart now, do yer?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, I fell over\'97I fell on him. I\'92m sorry.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yeah, yeah,\'92 the Coroner guys said. \'91Tax off, pal.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony taxed off, pushing his cart awkwardly with one hand while holding his overalls pocket tight, lest his loot spill out. He made for the safety of his van, illegally parked a few blocks more in a dead-end alley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Once behind the wheel, Tony dug into his pocket and fished out the meagre spoils. Among them was what looked like a coin-sized chad of thick, grainy, carbon-coloured leather. In Tony\'92s fingers, the disc was stiffly flexible, and sprang slovenly back into shape when he stopped bending it. For a moment, he thought he saw it flash pink. He waited to catch it again, but it stayed inert. He looked out the windshield, up towards the sky. The Layer was always playing tricks with the light.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He tossed the trinket on the passenger-side floor and coaxed the van\'92s motor into life.
